Note on 正日还库 (Return to the Treasury on the Proper Day):
This inscription is functionally significant. The 阴财神 shrine at this temple has a specific ritual calendar: on the 正日 (proper day — typically a specific lunar calendar date annually), devotees perform a 还库 (huán kù, "Return to the Treasury") ritual, in which debts or credit balances from the Yin realm are settled. This is a distinct ritual practice in which devotees who have borrowed from the underworld treasury (through an earlier petition to 阴财神) formally repay the debt.
This is more specific ritual practice than documented at Loyang Tua Pek Kong, where 阴财神 was simply analyzed functionally. The 还库 ritual makes the two-way transactional character of 阴财神's role explicit: devotees do not merely give offerings to 阴财神 (one-way transmittal); they also formally settle balances (two-way transaction).
