Zone 2 — 聚宝堂 ritual space
Image 4
- Large red banner on white tiled wall
- Large yellow characters on the banner
  (three main characters, with smaller text below)
- Flanking objects: vases with peacock feathers
- Incense burner placed on the FLOOR (not elevated)
- Fruit offerings at floor level (pineapple, bananas,
  oranges)
- Candle at floor level
- No deity figure or statue present
The floor-level placement. This is the most significant iconographic evidence. A 聚宝堂 wealth-gathering space would typically have some visual wealth symbolism and could be at any height. A 五方五土龙神 shrine is characteristically addressed at ground level — because these are the dragon spirits of the earth beneath, who govern the soil, and the ritual logic positions their incense burner directly on the ground surface they inhabit. The floor-level incense burner is a spatial coding that reads as 五方五土龙神, not 聚宝堂.
The absence of a deity figure. 五方五土龙神 is frequently addressed through a text invocation on red paper or banner rather than a sculptural figure. The banner functions as the spirit seat. This is why there is no statue — the banner IS the presence. A 聚宝堂 would more typically have symbolic visual elements (a basin, coins, auspicious objects). This shrine has none of those.
