The three-bay programme of Mun San Fook Tuck Chee encodes a specific Cantonese institutional theology:
LEFT BAY (金花夫人 + 十二奶嫲 + 奶姑 + associated)
  LIFE-BEGINNING dimension:
  Fertility, childbirth, infant welfare.
  The deities who govern the entry of new life into
  the world and its first years of development.
  Specifically Cantonese — marks community identity.

CENTRE BAY (Tu Di Gong)
  LIFE-SUSTAINING dimension:
  Earth, land, community, prosperity, protection
  in the living world.
  The deity who governs daily life, agriculture,
  commerce, and community affairs.
  Universal to all Chinese folk-religious traditions.

RIGHT BAY (Guan Yin)
  LIFE-TRANSCENDING dimension:
  Compassion, mercy, liberation from suffering.
  The bodhisattva who transcends the cycle of
  daily life toward spiritual liberation.
  Universal Buddhist — trans-traditional.

TOGETHER:
  Birth (left) → Life (center) → Liberation (right)

A life-course theology encoded in a three-bay
spatial programme: the temple addresses the full
arc of human existence from birth through daily
sustenance to transcendence.
This is the corpus's most explicitly life-course iconographic programme — where the three bays address three stages of human existence rather than three parallel functional domains.
